UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 12, Lesson 3: Hyperbole and idioms', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Hyperbole exaggerates for effect; idioms are common phrases whose meaning is not literal. Readers distinguish figurative intent from factual claims.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nIs \"My backpack weighs a tonne\" likely hyperbole or a measured fact? Explain.\n\nFEEDBACK\nHyperbole for emphasis unless an actual scale reading is given.\n\nCHECK\nTaking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCollect five hyperboles and five idioms (original or known). Rewrite two idioms in plain literal English and explain one hyperbole’s effect.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Hyperbole exaggerates for effect; idioms are common phrases whose meaning is not literal. Readers distinguish figurative intent from factual claims.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and spelling","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-VOCAB-01","EN2-SPELL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Collect five hyperboles and five idioms (original or known). Rewrite two idioms in plain literal English and explain one hyperbole’s effect. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Hyperbole for emphasis unless an actual scale reading is given. Reasoning: I used hyperbole by attending to idiom rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Taking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading. Correction: Hyperbole for emphasis unless an actual scale reading is given. Why: the correction restores accurate use of hyperbole.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Taking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating hyperbole on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with hyperbole, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Hyperbole for emphasis unless an actual scale reading is given. This matches the lesson skill for hyperbole.","options":["Taking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Hyperbole for emphasis unless an actual scale reading is given."],"question":"Is \"My backpack weighs a tonne\" likely hyperbole or a measured fact? Explain. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Taking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Taking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading.","Hyperbole exaggerates for effect; idioms are common phrases whose meaning is not literal. Readers distinguish figurative intent from factual claims.","Hyperbole for emphasis unless an actual scale reading is given.","Careful use of idiom with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising hyperbole?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named idiom, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the idiom used."],"question":"A learner is practising hyperbole. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries hyperbole.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show hyperbole and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Taking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading."],"question":"Read: \"In the dusty shed, Lila oiled the bike chain before the race.\" Which choice best uses hyperbole from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Hyperbole for emphasis unless an actual scale reading is given.","Taking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about hyperbole?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise idiom?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Taking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about hyperbole.","Retype the worked model as the final answer."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of hyperbole to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating hyperbole on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Hyperbole for emphasis unless an actual scale reading is given. Reasoning: I used hyperbole by attending to idiom rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with hyperbole, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Taking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading. Correction: Hyperbole for emphasis unless an actual scale reading is given. Why: the correction restores accurate use of hyperbole.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w12-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise hyperbole.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use hyperbole with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify idiom and explain why it matters.","I can practise literal independently on a new prompt.","I can notice figurative needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Hyperbole exaggerates for effect; idioms are common phrases whose meaning is not literal. Readers distinguish figurative intent from factual claims. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Hyperbole exaggerates for effect; idioms are common phrases whose meaning is not literal. Readers distinguish figurative intent from factual claims.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about hyperbole?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nHyperbole: \"I waited a thousand years for the bell.\" Idiom: \"break the ice\" means start a friendly talk, not smash frozen water.\n\nTRY FIRST\nIs \"My backpack weighs a tonne\" likely hyperbole or a measured fact? Explain.\n\nFEEDBACK\nHyperbole for emphasis unless an actual scale reading is given.","explain":"Hyperbole exaggerates for effect; idioms are common phrases whose meaning is not literal. Readers distinguish figurative intent from factual claims.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Taking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Hyperbole and idiom as figurative language.","caption":"Not every phrase is literal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Hyperbole</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">waited a thousand years</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Idiom</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">break the ice</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Hyperbole: \"I waited a thousand years for the bell.\" Idiom: \"break the ice\" means start a friendly talk, not smash froze Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Hyperbole: \"I waited a thousand years for the bell.\" Idiom: \"break the ice\" means start a friendly talk, not smash froze Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about hyperbole?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nHyperbole: \"I waited a thousand years for the bell.\" Idiom: \"break the ice\" means start a friendly talk, not smash frozen water. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Hyperbole effect contrasted with literal idiom mistakes.","caption":"Rewrite idioms in plain English to check meaning.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Effect</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">emphasis by exaggeration</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">literal idiom reading</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about hyperbole?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTaking every idiom literally creates silly misunderstandings in reading. Here is a more productive model: Hyperbole: \"I waited a thousand years for the bell.\" Idiom: \"break the ice\" means start a friendly talk, not smash frozen water.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nHyperbole for emphasis unless an actual scale reading is given.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then 